<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djemal Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Djemal Pasha, Cemal Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djemal Pasha was an Ottoman officer and politician, one of the three Young Turk leaders who governed the empire during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. As commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1914 he led failed attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, abolished the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and executed Arab nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, earning the name 'the Butcher'. He fled after the war and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha.jpg" alt="Djemal Pasha" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djemal_Pasha2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First World War closed the age with censorship, famine in Mount Lebanon and, in 1915–1916, the hanging of Arab nationalists in Beirut and Damascus under <b>Djemal Pasha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Talaat Pasha and <b>Cemal Pasha</b> were assassinated in exile during Operation Nemesis, an Armenian revenge operation: Soghomon Tehlirian, whose family had been killed in the genocide, shot Talaat in Berlin and was acquitted by a German jury.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1915 <b>Jamal Pasha</b> ended the autonomy; wartime blockade and famine killed a large part of the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-mutasarrifate">Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate</a></li></ul></section>
